s, including certain cancers, obesity, type 2 diabetes, and cardiovascular diseases. \n\nFiber is essential in pediatric nutrition, often included in formulas to support digestion and health. It is also beneficial for individuals with hereditary fructose intolerance, as it is commonly found in foods that are otherwise restricted. Additionally, fiber can aid in the absorption of water and electrolytes, and it may help prevent diarrhea in enteral nutrition patients.\n\nThe recommended daily intake of dietary fiber is between 20 to 35 grams, which is important for preventing constipation in elderly patients and improving glycemic control and cholesterol levels. Furthermore, fiber has been associated with lower levels of uric acid and a reduced risk of hyperuricemia, making it a valuable component for individuals with diabetes. Overall, FIBRA is recognized for its protective effects against various diseases and its essential role in maintaining digestive health.\",25\r\n4119,DIABETES TIPO 2,\"Diabetes tipo 2 is a chronic medical condition that has affected the patient for 12 years, primarily associated with obesity and challenges in glucose control. This condition significantly impacts how the body processes blood sugar (glucose) and is characterized by insulin resistance and elevated blood sugar levels. It is often linked to various lifestyle factors, including dietary habits such as the consumption of sugary beverages and trans fats, as well as the intake of fiber and whole grains, which may help reduce the risk of developing the disease.\n\nDiabetes tipo 2 is prevalent in Spain and poses a considerable burden on healthcare resources and costs. It is also associated with an increased risk of complications, including cardiovascular disease and uric acid stone formation. The condition can be treated with medications such as liraglutide and may require insulin in some cases. Additionally, diabetes tipo 2 is related to metabolic syndrome and has shown an inverse association with vitamin D levels.\n\nThe rising prevalence of diabetes tipo 2, particularly among older populations, highlights the importance of addressing lifestyle factors and dietary choices to manage and potentially prevent this condition. Furthermore, it is considered in the context of metabolic surgery for obese patients and is often discussed in relation to low-carbohydrate diets and very low-calorie diets (VLCD). Overall, diabetes tipo 2 is a complex metabolic disorder that necessitates a comprehensive approach to treatment and management.\",23\r\n5343,SÍNDROME METABÓLICO,\"The \"\"Síndrome Metabólico\"\" is a complex cluster of conditions that significantly increases the risk of heart disease, stroke, and diabetes. This syndrome encompasses several interrelated factors, including hypertension, hyperglycemia, excess abdominal fat, and abnormal cholesterol levels. It is often associated with insulin resistance, obesity, and poor nutrition, and can be exacerbated by lifestyle choices such as high salt consumption and the intake of sugar-sweetened beverages and trans fats.\n\nAdditionally, the syndrome may be influenced by dietary factors, including protein and dairy consumption, although evidence regarding the protective effects of dairy is inconsistent. There is also a noted association between the syndrome and conditions such as dislipidemia, hyperuricemia, and kidney stone formation. Furthermore, treatment with testosterone has been identified as a potential exacerbating factor for the syndrome.\n\nOverall, the \"\"Síndrome Metabólico\"\" represents a significant health concern, necessitating careful dietary management and lifestyle modifications to mitigate its associated risks.\",21\r\n3172,ENFERMEDADES CARDIOVASCULARES,\"\"\"ENFERMEDADES CARDIOVASCULARES\"\" refers to a group of disorders that affect the heart and blood vessels, encompassing a wide range of health conditions. These diseases are significantly influenced by various factors, including dietary choices, lifestyle habits, and nutritional intake. Key dietary influences include vitamin D levels, the consumption of salt, dairy products, trans fats, and sugary beverages, as well as the balance of fats and carbohydrates in the diet. \n\nCardiovascular diseases are particularly concerning for patients with chronic kidney disease, where phosphorus levels play a critical role. They are also major complications associated with diabetes, which can increase mortality risk. Furthermore, these diseases are linked to other serious health issues, such as Alzheimer's disease and other dementias, highlighting their broad impact on overall health.\n\nIn Western countries, cardiovascular diseases are the leading cause of death and disability, often associated with modifiable risk factors like hypertension and type 2 diabetes. As such, they represent one of the primary health threats in the 21st century. Preventative measures through improved nutrition and lifestyle changes are essential in mitigating the risks associated with these conditions.\n\nAdditionally, \"\"ENFERMEDADES CARDIOVASCULARES\"\" is a focus area within one of the 17 thematic research networks under RETICS, emphasizing the importance of ongoing research and intervention strategies in addressing cardiovascular health.\",22\r\n7311,CEREALES INTEGRALES,\"Cereales integrales, or whole grains, are a vital source of dietary fiber, vitamins, minerals, and phytochemicals, which contribute to improved health and a reduced risk of various diseases. Their consumption has been linked to a lower risk of colorectal cancer and type 2 diabetes, highlighting their anticancer properties. Whole grains are particularly beneficial for cardiovascular health, as they are associated with a decreased risk of cardiovascular disease and related events. They are also a good source of magnesium, especially when the bran is included.\n\nHowever, it is important to note that some whole grain cereals can fragment easily, which may pose a risk for individuals with swallowing difficulties. Additionally, there are concerns regarding the high phosphorus content in certain whole grains, such as brown rice, which can reach up to 40 mg/g. Despite these considerations, the overall health benefits of consuming whole grains are widely recognized, making them a recommended component of a balanced diet.\",9\r\n7330,BEBIDAS AZUCARADAS,\"BEBIDAS AZUCARADAS, or sugary drinks, are beverages that contain added sugars. They are associated with an increased risk of metabolic and cardiovascular diseases. Dietary recommendations discourage the consumption of sugary drinks to promote better health outcomes. Furthermore, these drinks are highlighted in dietary guidelines, which advise limiting their intake for health reasons.\",7\r\n6141,GRASAS TRANS,\"GRASAS TRANS, or trans fats, are a type of fat that is generally avoided in the Mediterranean diet due to their harmful effects on health. These fats have been linked to an increased risk of cardiovascular diseases and other health issues. Dietary guidelines recommend avoiding trans fats because of their negative impact on heart health.\",6\r\n6136,SOCIETAD ESPAÑOLA DE ARTERIOSCLEROSIS,\"The \"\"Sociedad Española de Arteriosclerosis\"\" is an organization dedicated to promoting cardiovascular health through the establishment of dietary recommendations. It provides guidelines and classifications for foods, specifically focusing on their impact on lipid levels. This dual approach emphasizes the importance of nutrition in managing and preventing atherosclerosis and related cardiovascular conditions.\",2\r\n7861,ALIMENTOS RICOS EN FIBRA,\"Foods high in fiber, especially fruits and vegetables, are associated with a probable decrease in the risk of several diseases.\",1\r\n3441,SENSIBILIDAD PERIFÉRICA A LA INSULINA,\"Peripheral insulin sensitivity is a metabolic condition that can be improved through dietary choices, particularly those high in fiber and complex carbohydrates.\",1\r\n10